.TP
.B watch ls
Refresh a directory listing every two seconds.
.TP
.B watch \-n 0.5 free \-h
Watch memory usage twice per second. \fB\-n\fR accepts fractional
seconds.
.TP
.B watch \-t uptime
Watch the system's load average without the header line, so the
single line of output centers nicely.
.TP
.B watch 'ls \-la /tmp/incoming | wc \-l'
Pipe and redirection work because the default execution path is
\fBsh \-c\fR. Quote the whole command to keep it together.
.TP
.B watch \-g 'ls /var/spool/jobs'
Exit as soon as the directory contents change. Useful for waiting
on a file to appear or disappear.
.TP
.B watch \-q 5 systemctl is\-active foo.service
Exit successfully once the command's output has been stable for five
consecutive iterations.
.TP
.B watch \-e ./flaky\-script
Freeze on error: as soon as the script returns non-zero, the display
stops updating and \fBwatch\fR waits for any single keypress before
exiting with status 1. Add \fB\-b\fR to beep.
.TP
.B watch \-x \-\- env \-i HOME=/root my\-tool \-\-flag
Skip the shell entirely with \fB\-x\fR; \fBwatch\fR \fBexec\fRs the
command directly. The \fB\-\-\fR separator prevents \fB\-\-flag\fR
from being parsed by \fBwatch\fR itself.
